@page "/printers"
@using Restaurant.UI.Shared.Components.Screens

@* Back office · Printers. Discover a printer over the network or the host's radio,
   choose one, and print a test label.

   **WHOSE RADIO AND WHOSE NETWORK.** Restaurant.Blazor is Blazor Server: the C#
   behind this page runs in the ASP.NET process, not in the browser. So what this
   page scans is the machine serving the back office — the counter PC, the mini-PC
   in the office, the POS device itself where the back office is served from it —
   and never the laptop the manager happens to be reading this on. That is not a
   limitation to be hidden; it is the fact that decides whether a scan finds
   anything, so the screen states it in a line rather than leaving somebody to work
   it out from an empty list.

   **Why this reuses PrinterSetupScreen rather than getting its own.** The job is
   the same job — list what can be reached, choose one, prove it prints — and every
   state on it is one a transport can prove. What differs between the two hosts is
   six sentences and whether a typed address is offered, and those are parameters.
   Building a second screen would mean two places to keep the seven states honest
   in, which is exactly how a NOT TESTED becomes a READY nobody earned.

   What is genuinely different is the frame: the terminal's screen is inside
   TerminalShell with its 76px bar and 78px nav, and this one is a back-office
   .panel in the rail layout. That is this page's job and not the screen's.

   **Opening this page scans nothing.** A scan here is a multicast query and, where
   that finds nothing, a bounded sweep of the subnet — and doing that on every page
   open, or every time a Blazor circuit reconnects, is exactly what a restaurant
   network must not get. It waits to be asked. The terminal keeps its scan on open,
   because reading a bonded list the platform already holds costs the room nothing.

   This page does NOT record which terminal claims which printer. That is the
   Devices registry, it is a new table, and it is deliberately the next task and
   not this one. What is selected here is held for as long as this server process
   and no longer, and the screen says nothing that implies otherwise. *@

<PageTitle>Printers</PageTitle>

<section class="panel">
    <PrinterSetupScreen TerminalId="Back office"
                        Kicker="BACK OFFICE · PRINTERS"
                        Note="@Note"
                        ListLabel="PRINTERS THIS HOST CAN REACH"
                        EmptyHint="@EmptyHint"
                        HostNote="@HostNote"
                        ScanOnOpen="false"
                        ShowLimits="false" />
</section>

@code {
    private const string Note =
        "Printers this back office can reach, over the venue network and over the radio of the " +
        "machine serving this page.";

    private const string HostNote =
        "These are the network and the radio of the machine serving the back office, not of the " +
        "computer you are reading this on. A printer beside you is only found here if this page is " +
        "served from the machine beside it.";

    private const string EmptyHint =
        "Nothing found yet. Check the printer is powered on and on the same network as this host, " +
        "then scan — or add it by the address on its self-test page.";
}
